_total": 7.5, "baseline_margin": -10.79, "analyst_adjustment": -0.21, "key_factors": ["R+11 district lean makes the Upper Peninsula seat solid Republican terrain", "All ten raters place the race Safe/Solid or Likely R, and markets price Bergman near 89 percent", "Bergman holds the fundraising lead and has already defeated Barr once in this rematch", "No public polling; Barr's cash-on-hand edge and talk of an expanded battlefield are the main upside hints"], "rationale": "The district's eleven-point Republican lean, Bergman's incumbency and fundraising edge, a rating consensus of Safe or Solid R, and markets pricing Bergman around 90 percent all point to a comfortable hold, and no polling evidence suggests Barr has narrowed the gap.", "watch": "New polling showing Barr within single digits, or a sharply Democratic national environment in the final weeks, would be the clearest sign of a genuine contest.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Jack Bergman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Northern Michigan's First District is firmly Republican territory, with a partisan lean of roughly eleven points toward the GOP. Representative Jack Bergman is seeking re-election in a rematch against Democrat Callie Barr, whom he defeated in 2024. Rating outlets overwhelmingly place the race in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and prediction markets give Bergman roughly an 89 percent chance of holding the seat.\n\nBergman holds the fundraising lead, having raised about $2.3 million to Barr's $1.5 million, though Barr ends with slightly more cash on hand. No public polls of the general election exist, so expectations rest on district fundamentals and expert assessments. Democratic hopes of an expanded 2026 battlefield hinge on national currents overcoming the district's heavy Republican tilt, which current evidence does not suggest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0479, "